    <!--
        THE FULL RATIONALE LIVES HERE, NOT IN <description>. A comment is repo-only and is never
        sent to the Metadata API, so it carries what will not fit. It must sit INSIDE the root
        element - a comment placed ABOVE <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion
        with the misleading error "unable to find matching parent xml file".

        =====================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-14 (Phase 3, observation 5). THE "FULLY DISJOINT" CLAIM THIS FIELD
        MADE - AND THAT FIVE OTHER FILES RELIED ON - IS NOW FALSE. READ THIS BEFORE CITING IT.
        =====================================================================

        RETRACTED, verbatim from the <description> this change replaced: "The two sets are FULLY
        DISJOINT, so unlike NDA__c.Status__c this field alone DOES identify the record type -
        which is what makes every stage-keyed flexipage rule self-limiting with no record-type
        criterion."

        WHY IT IS NOW FALSE. The acquisition sequence becomes
        Draft -> Under Review -> Submitted -> Negotiation -> Signed, and 'Under Review' ALREADY
        EXISTED as step 2 of the DISPOSITION sequence. It is now carried by BOTH record types.
        No value was renamed to produce this - 'Under Review' is REUSED rather than added, which
        is exactly why the overlap is easy to miss when reading the diff.

        ⚠ WHAT THAT COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE REDISCOVERED. Every rule anywhere that
        keys on a Stage__c VALUE and relies on the value alone to identify the record type must
        be re-read. Four artefacts made that reliance explicitly and all four were amended in
        this change: both recordTypes/*.recordType-meta.xml files, LOI_Path_Acquisition's
        header, and flexipages/LOI_Record_Page's header. A fifth, Is_Advance_Allowed__c, carried
        an explicit instruction NOT to add a record-type test on exactly this premise; that
        instruction is retracted in that file and the formula now carries one.

        ⚠ THE OVERLAP IS ON A NON-TERMINAL VALUE, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT SURVIVABLE.
        The two terminals stay distinct - 'Signed' on acquisition, 'Executed' on disposition -
        so every terminal-state test can still be written by naming values. It is the MIDDLE of
        the sequence that is now ambiguous, and the one live consequence is the approval gate:
        an acquisition LOI at 'Under Review' is awaiting approval, a disposition LOI at
        'Under Review' is being read by DPEG and has no approval concept at all.
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-21, verbatim: "The two terminals stay distinct - 'Signed' on
        acquisition, 'Executed' on disposition - so every terminal-state test can still be
        written by naming values." Disposition_LOI's terminal moved to 'Signed' (user request);
        BOTH record types now terminate on the same value. See the master retraction block near
        the end of this comment for the full consequence and how the one live terminal-state test
        on this object, Is_Advance_Allowed__c, survived it anyway.

        ORDERING - CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED [ARITHMETIC BELOW WAS CORRECT 2026-08-14 TO 2026-08-21;
        SEE THE MASTER RETRACTION NEAR THE END OF THIS COMMENT FOR THE CURRENT RENDERING]. Runtime
        Path order comes from THIS file's value order (sorted=false) filtered by the record type;
        a record type can only include or exclude, never re-order. The three new values are
        APPENDED (positions 11-13), which yields:
          Acquisition_LOI  -> Draft(1), Under Review(7), Submitted(11), Negotiation(12), Signed(13)
          Disposition_LOI  -> Received(6), Under Review(7), Countered by DPEG(8),
                              Counter Received from Buyer(9), Executed(10)   [UNCHANGED]
        Both are monotonic and correct, so an APPEND is sufficient here and no interleave is
        needed. That is luck, not a rule: it holds only because the one shared value sits at a
        position that is correct for both sequences. ⚠ CONTRAST Contract_Review__c's
        Negotiation_Status__c in this same phase, where the shared value ('Executed') sits at
        position 4 and an append would have rendered the TERMINAL step FIRST - so the three new
        values there had to be INSERTED. Anyone adding a value to either field must redo this
        arithmetic; do not carry the answer across.

        THE FOUR RETIRED ACQUISITION VALUES ARE DEACTIVATED, NOT DELETED (decision O3).
        'Prepare/Review', 'Sent', 'Counter' and 'Completed' go inactive at Phase 3 D3, AFTER the
        row migration. Deletion buys nothing an inactive value has not already delivered and
        breaks reports, dashboards and Paths silently. Every one of the four is also a
        substring-collision hazard: 'Completed' is ACTIVE on Underwriting__c.Stage__c and on
        both Feasibility Review Stage__c fields; 'Sent' is ACTIVE on NDA__c.Status__c for BOTH
        record types; 'Counter' is a strict prefix of 'Countered by DPEG' and
        'Counter Received from Buyer' ON THIS VERY FIELD; and 'Prepare/Review' carries a literal
        slash, stored ENCODED as Prepare%2FReview inside a RecordType and LITERALLY everywhere
        else. Any sweep for these values must be FIELD-scoped, never string-scoped.

        ⚠ COMPARISONS ON THIS FIELD USE TEXT(...), NEVER ISPICKVAL - RecordStageAdvanceService's
        class header is the standing rule and 'Prepare/Review' is the reason. That rule outlives
        the value: keep using TEXT() after D3.

        ⚠ objectTranslations/LOI__c-en_US/Stage__c.fieldTranslation-meta.xml enumerates only the
        FIVE ORIGINAL acquisition values. The five disposition values added in Tranche 3B were
        never added to it and nothing broke, so the three added here are deliberately not added
        either - see the runbook's translation note. That file is NOT part of any Phase 3 deploy.

        ================================================================================
        🔴 READ THIS BEFORE REASONING FROM "THE PSA CASE" - PHASE 3 SWAPPED THE TWO OBJECTS'
        PROPERTIES, SO THE ANALOGY NOW POINTS AT THE INVERSE OF THE FACTS.
        ================================================================================

        This field and Contract_Review__c.Negotiation_Status__c are the sibling pair, and repo
        prose has long reasoned from one to the other. On 2026-08-14 they EXCHANGED the property
        every one of those arguments rests on:

            BEFORE 2026-08-14                      AFTER
            LOI__c.Stage__c (this)  DISJOINT  ->   OVERLAPS at 'Under Review'
                                                       [FROM P3-D1]
            Negotiation_Status__c   IDENTICAL ->   DISJOINT except the terminal 'Executed'
                                                       [ONLY FROM P3-D3]

        🔴 THE TWO HALVES DO NOT LAND TOGETHER, AND THE WINDOW BETWEEN THEM IS THE NEAR-TERM
        STATE - NOTHING IN THIS PACK IS DEPLOYED YET. The LOI overlap arrives at P3-D1, the
        moment the three new values deploy. The PSA disjointness arrives only at P3-D3, when
        Acquisition_PSA stops exposing 'Initial Draft', 'Revised' and 'Ready for Execution' -
        all three of which Disposition_PSA also carries, and all three of which it keeps.
        FOR THE WHOLE D1 -> D3 WINDOW THE TWO PSA SETS ARE NOT DISJOINT AND A STATUS-KEYED RULE
        ON Contract_Review__c IS NOT SELF-LIMITING. Reading the row above as if it took effect
        on 2026-08-14 inverts the fact for exactly the period this block will be read in, which
        is the opposite of the job it exists to do. flexipages/Contract_Review_Record_Page states
        the same qualifier: "from P3-D3 a rule keyed on any NON-terminal status value here WOULD
        be self-limiting". The LOI row needs no such qualifier - its overlap is additive and so
        arrives with the values themselves, at D1.

        WHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA. "The sets are disjoint, so a rule keyed on a
        stage value is SELF-LIMITING to its record type" was TRUE here and FALSE of the PSA; it
        is FALSE here from P3-D1 and TRUE of the PSA only from P3-D3. Carrying that sentence in
        either direction after
        this date carries the exact opposite of the facts, and it fails SILENTLY - a rule that
        stops being self-limiting renders a button on the wrong record type, and one that becomes
        self-limiting hides it. Neither errors and no test sees it.

        WHAT IT COST ALREADY: LOI_Record_Page's Submit_for_Approval entry keys on 'Under Review',
        which is now shared, and carries an OPEN, UNDECIDED residual (runbook 1.6, gate G7). It
        is also why Is_Advance_Allowed__c's approval clause needed a RecordType.DeveloperName
        guard - the ONLY clause in that formula that has one.

        ⚠ THE TERMINALS ARE THE PART THAT DID NOT MOVE, on either object: 'Signed' vs 'Executed'
        here, and 'Executed' shared but sole-terminal on the PSA. That is precisely why every
        terminal-state test still works by naming values while the MIDDLE of the sequence does
        not - the overlap is mid-sequence, which is the harder case to notice.

        ⇒ RULE: never inherit a disjointness claim across this object pair. Re-derive it from the
        two record type files at the moment you need it. It has already inverted once.

